Croatian law does not mention hitchhiking (''autostop''), so on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://narodne-novine.nn.hr/clanci/sluzbeni/2008_06_67_2224.html Article 139(1) of the ''Zakon o sigurnosti prometa na cestama''] bars pedestrians — with horse-drawn vehicles, bicycles, animals and anything unable to exceed 60 km/h — from an ''autocesta'', a ''brza cesta'' and any road reserved for motor vehicles, which takes in the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip road. Article 140(1) lets a driver stop or park on an ''autocesta'' only on areas off the carriageway specially built and marked for it, and 140(3) forbids U-turns and reversing, so a driver who passes you cannot come back. That leaves the ''odmorišta'' and their petrol stations as the one place on the motorway where a lift can lawfully begin. On ''državne'' and ''županijske ceste'' thumbing from the verge breaks no rule.
